{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1927 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "14cf3047bbab88eb40aba7f272e3ca810a245e33084269108c6ac28db1054109" :edb/db-before-t 1926 :edb/db-after-t 1927 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535239 50 #inst "2026-10-08T20:12:06.120Z" 13194139535239 true] [17592186124271 1100 "There are two groups of people in a particular city: those who use their own vehicles and those who use public transport. A study has shown that, each year\n\n- $35\\%$ of people who used their own vehicles swap to public transport, and\n\n- $25\\%$ of people who used public transport swap to their own vehicles.\n\nGive the stochastic matrix that describes how people change their means of transportation each year." 13194139535239 true] [17592186124271 1100 "There are two groups of people in a particular city: those who use their own vehicles and those who use public transport. A study has shown that, each year\n\n- $35\\%$ of people who used their own vehicles swap to public transport, and\n- $25\\%$ of people who used public transport swap to their own vehicles.\n\nGive the stochastic matrix that describes how people change their means of transportation each year." 13194139535239 false] [17592186124271 1145 "We are given the following situation for each year:\n\n- $35\\%$ of people who used their own vehicles swap to public transport. This implies that $65\\%$ of them remain using their own vehicles.\n- $25\\%$ of people who used public transport swap to their own vehicles. This implies that $75\\%$ of them remain using public transport.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | **Vehicles** | **Public Transport** |\n| To: | **Vehicles** | $0.65$ | $0.25$ |\n| **Public Transport** | $0.35$ | $0.75$ |  |\n\nNote that we can write a corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0.65 & 0.25 \\\\ 0.35 & 0.75\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535239 false] [17592186124271 1145 "We are given the following situation for each year: \n\n- $35\\%$ of people who used their own vehicles swap to public transport. This implies that $65\\%$ of them remain using their own vehicles.\n\n- $25\\%$ of people who used public transport swap to their own vehicles. This implies that $75\\%$ of them remain using public transport.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Vehicles** | **Public Transport ** |\n| To: | **Vehicles** | $0.65$ | $0.25$ |\n| **Public Transport** | $0.35$ | $0.75$ |  |\n\nNote that we can write a corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/assets/b21fee1abe56dcfdc687be14278c62a20d201ff00e96f1e5918ce22d9dc8b03c.png)" 13194139535239 true] [17592186124272 1100 "A certain industry produces $2$ types of sugar, namely sugar $A$ and sugar $B$. The following table describes how the number of consumers changes between both types of sugar in a month:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Sugar $A$** | **Sugar $B$** |\n| To: | **Sugar $A$** | $0.3$ | $0.45$ |\n| **Sugar $B$** | $0.7$ | $0.55$ |  |\n\nThat is, $70\\%$ of sugar $A$ customers switch to sugar $B$ each month, and $45\\%$ of sugar $B$ customers switch to sugar $A.$\n\nCurrently, $600$ customers use sugar $A,$ while $400$ customers use sugar $B.$ How many customers will use sugar $A$ in one month? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." 13194139535239 true] [17592186124272 1100 "A certain industry produces $2$ types of sugar, namely sugar $A$ and sugar $B$. The following table describes how the number of consumers changes between both types of sugar in a month:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | **Sugar $A$** | **Sugar $B$** |\n| To: | **Sugar $A$** | $0.3$ | $0.45$ |\n| **Sugar $B$** | $0.7$ | $0.55$ |  |\n\nThat is, $70\\%$ of sugar $A$ customers switch to sugar $B$ each month, and $45\\%$ of sugar $B$ customers switch to sugar $A$.\n\nCurrently, $600$ customers use sugar $A$, while $400$ customers use sugar $B$. How many customers will use sugar $A$ in one month? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." 13194139535239 false] [17592186124272 1145 "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.3 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.55\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of sugar is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}600 \\\\ 400\\end{bmatrix}$\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000)$, we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}600 \\\\ 400\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector $\\mathbf{x}_1$ after one month, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_1=P\\mathbf{x}_0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= Px_{0} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0.3 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.55\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.6 \\\\ 0.4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0.36 \\\\ 0.64\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one month, $\\color{blue}36\\%$ of customers will use sugar $A$. Computing $36\\%$ of $1\\,000$, we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000 \\cdot 0.36 = 360\n$$\n\nTherefore, $360$ customers will use sugar $A$ in one month." 13194139535239 false] [17592186124272 1145 "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/assets/451b82e8cb00ede7b0ddf73f35c0555d0eeeeefc5beb5ce4fddd54f0df5f2577.png)\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of sugar is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/assets/56952aeed1d718033af8a3e6d09e8e269f1d70aa8dc31609b8289f025f2e6bd6.png)\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/assets/cae1bed6b8b01b3ff66c2e8bacf7a10f6c0ece53f3aeac0fb1a51e8fc3c07e8e.png)\n\nTo find the state vector $\\mathbf{x}_1$ after one month, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_1=P\\mathbf{x}_0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.3 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.55\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.6 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.36 \\\\ 0.64\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one month, $\\color{blue}36\\%$ of customers will use sugar $A.$ Computing $36\\%$ of $1\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000 \\cdot 0.36 = 360.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $360$ customers will use sugar $A$ in one month." 13194139535239 true] [17592186124281 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/assets/165917feacd28344502759fc6e03f96c5b54d9530701916b746fc489374a6d0b.png) \n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector $\\mathbf{x}_0$ given above. Calculate the state vector $\\mathbf{x}_2.$" 13194139535239 true] [17592186124281 1100 "$\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.9\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector $\\mathbf{x}_0$ given above. Calculate the state vector $\\mathbf{x}_2$." 13194139535239 false] [17592186124281 1145 "To find the state vector $\\mathbf{x}_2$, we apply two steps of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x_1}=P\\mathbf{x}_0, \\qquad \\mathbf{x}_2=P\\mathbf{x}_1\n$$\n\nAfter the first step, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= Px_{0} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0.5 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0.18 \\\\ 0.82\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, after the second step, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= Px_{1} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0.5 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.18 \\\\ 0.82\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0.172 \\\\ 0.828\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535239 false] [17592186124281 1145 "To find the state vector $\\mathbf{x}_2,$ we apply two steps of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x_1}=P\\mathbf{x}_0, \\qquad \\mathbf{x}_2=P\\mathbf{x}_1\n$$\n\nAfter the first step, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.18 \\\\ 0.82\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, after the second step, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.18 \\\\ 0.82\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.172 \\\\ 0.828\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535239 true] [17592186124343 1100 "Which of the following are stochastic matrices?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/assets/70ee929c71d89a443d3ea0efc39ed225f344685059b86a04285a118a476da0f8.png)" 13194139535239 true] [17592186124343 1100 "Which of the following are stochastic matrices?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0 \\\\ 1.2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535239 false] [17592186124343 1145 "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1$. A matrix is stochastic if all its columns are stochastic vectors.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices in turn.\n\n- Matrix $\\bf{U}$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its first column is not a stochastic vector (it contains negative entries):\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0 \\\\ 1.2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Matrix $\\bf{V}$ is a stochastic matrix. Indeed, each column is a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries are nonnegative and add up to one):\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0.35 & 0.95 \\\\ 0.65 & 0.05\\end{bmatrix}0.35 + 0.65 = 1; 0.95 + 0.05 = 1$\n- Matrix $\\bf{W}$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its third column is not a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries don't add up to one):\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 0 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.5 & 0 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\bf{V}$ only.\"" 13194139535239 false] [17592186124343 1145 "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$ A matrix is stochastic if all its columns are stochastic vectors.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices in turn.\n\n- Matrix $\\bf{U}$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its first column is not a stochastic vector (it contains negative entries): \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 0 \\\\ 1.2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Matrix $\\bf{V}$ is a stochastic matrix. Indeed, each column is a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries are nonnegative and add up to one): \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}0.35 & 0.95 \\\\ 0.65 & 0.05\\end{aligned}]\\,\\begin{aligned}0.35+0.65=1 \\\\ 0.95+0.05=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Matrix $\\bf{W}$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its third column is not a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries don't add up to one): \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 0 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.5 & 0 & 0.7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\bf{V}$ only.\"" 13194139535239 true] [17592186124465 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339231 13194139535239 true] [17592186124465 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339238 13194139535239 true] [17592186124465 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339245 13194139535239 true] [17592186124465 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339252 13194139535239 true] [17592186124465 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339266 13194139535239 true] [17592186124466 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339175 13194139535239 true] [17592186124466 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339182 13194139535239 true] [17592186124466 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339189 13194139535239 true] [17592186124466 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339196 13194139535239 true] [17592186124475 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339154 13194139535239 true] [17592186124475 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339161 13194139535239 true] [17592186124475 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339168 13194139535239 true] [17592186124475 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339259 13194139535239 true] [17592186124535 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339203 13194139535239 true] [17592186124535 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339210 13194139535239 true] [17592186124535 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339217 13194139535239 true] [17592186124535 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339224 13194139535239 true] [17592186124686 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535239 true] [17592186124686 1100 "Which of the following are stochastic vectors?\n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0.5\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-0.45 \\\\ 1.55\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535239 true] [17592186124686 1100 "Which of the following are stochastic vectors?\n\n$u = \\begin{bmatrix}0.5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0.5\\end{bmatrix}, v = \\begin{bmatrix}-0.45 \\\\ 1.55\\end{bmatrix}, w = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535239 false] [17592186124686 1145 "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our vectors in turn.\n\n- Vector $u$ is a stochastic vector. Indeed, \n\n- its entries are all nonnegative, and\n\n- the entries add up to one:\n\n$$\n0.5+0+0.5=1\n$$\n\n- Vector $v$ is *not* a stochastic vector since it contains negative entries.\n\n- Vector $w$ is *not* a stochastic vector since the entries do not add up to one:\n\n$$\n0+0=0\\ne 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$u$ only.\"" 13194139535239 true] [17592186124840 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535239 true] [17592186124840 1100 "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA certain company produces two types of smartphones: Phone $A$ and Phone $B$. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between both types of smartphones each year:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | **Phone $A$** | **Phone $B$** |\n| To: | **Phone $A$** | $0.6$ | $0.3$ |\n| **Phone $B$** | $0.4$ | $0.7$ |  |\n\nThat is, $40%$ of Phone $A$ customers switch to Phone $B$ each year, and $30%$ of Phone $B$ customers switch to Phone $A$.\n\nCurrently, $600000$ customers use Phone $A$, while $400000$ customers use Phone $B$. How many customers will use Phone $A$ in one year? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." 13194139535239 false] [17592186124840 1100 "A certain company produces two types of smartphones: Phone $A$ and Phone $B.$ The following table describes how the number of customers changes between both types of smartphones each year:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Phone $A$** | **Phone $B$** |\n| To: | **Phone $A$** | $0.6$ | $0.3$ |\n| **Phone $B$** | $0.4$ | $0.7$ |  |\n\nThat is, $40%$ of Phone $A$ customers switch to Phone $B$ each year, and $30%$ of Phone $B$ customers switch to Phone $A.$\n\nCurrently, $600\\,000$ customers use Phone $A,$ while $400\\,000$ customers use Phone $B.$ How many customers will use Phone $A$ in one year? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." 13194139535239 true] [17592186124840 1145 "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.6 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each smartphone is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}600\\,000 \\\\ 400\\,000\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{1\\,000\\,000}[\\begin{aligned}600\\,000 \\\\ 400\\,000\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.6 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.6 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.6 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.48 \\\\ 0.52\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one year, $48%$ of customers will use Phone $A.$ Computing $48%$ of $1\\,000\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.48=480\\,000.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $480\\,000$ customers will use Phone $A$ in one year." 13194139535239 true] [17592186124841 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535239 true] [17592186124841 1100 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.5\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector ${x}_{0}$ given above. Calculate the state vector ${x}_{2}.$" 13194139535239 true] [17592186124841 1100 "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}0.2 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}, x_{0} = \\begin{bmatrix}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector $x_{0}$ given above. Calculate the state vector $x_{2}$." 13194139535239 false] [17592186124841 1145 "To find the state vector ${x}_{2},$ we apply two steps of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0},\\,{x}_{2}=P{x}_{1}\n$$\n\nAfter the first step, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.29 \\\\ 0.71\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, after the second step, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.29 \\\\ 0.71\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.413 \\\\ 0.587\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339154 1017 #edb/ref