{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 0.9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector ${x}_{0}$ given above. Calculate the state vector ${x}_{2}.$" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339168 1113 #uuid "b6f2bb21-8c66-5d1d-89c2-970e35eeb5ec" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339168 1145 "To find the state vector ${x}_{2},$ we apply two steps of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0},\\,{x}_{2}=P{x}_{1}\n$$\n\nAfter the first step, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 0.9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.38 \\\\ 0.62\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, after the second step, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.38 \\\\ 0.62\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.324 \\\\ 0.676\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339169 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339170 13194139535239 true] [17592186339169 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339171 13194139535239 true] [17592186339169 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339172 13194139535239 true] [17592186339169 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339173 13194139535239 true] [17592186339169 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339174 13194139535239 true] [17592186339169 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535239 true] [17592186339169 1041 "selection" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339169 1104 #edb/ref 17592186339171 13194139535239 true] [17592186339169 1110 #uuid "12f641fe-2871-5149-bfe0-0cb7ec41d57c" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339170 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}0.312 \\\\ 0.688\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339170 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535239 true] [17592186339170 1111 #uuid "93012f05-6416-511b-b5f4-05767a8f7dad" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339171 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}0.324 \\\\ 0.676\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339171 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535239 true] [17592186339171 1111 #uuid "6f9ebbd8-3581-55bc-a6a2-1601eb5fe0d3" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339172 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}0.434 \\\\ 0.566\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339172 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535239 true] [17592186339172 1111 #uuid "7301decc-e4cb-5420-b963-05c7c9305215" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339173 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}0.364 \\\\ 0.636\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339173 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535239 true] [17592186339173 1111 #uuid "d8505ec2-14fa-5c0a-81e2-7f7c097f6c59" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339174 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}0.296 \\\\ 0.704\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339174 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535239 true] [17592186339174 1111 #uuid "fb79c0ad-eae2-57e6-a5e0-02d3f28927ec" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339175 1017 #edb/ref 17592186339176 13194139535239 true] [17592186339175 1089 "q-135458" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339175 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535239 true] [17592186339175 1100 "A certain company produces two types of motorcycles: classic and high-cylinder. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between both types of motorcycles each decade:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Classic** | **High-Cylinder** |\n| To: | **Classic** | $0.6$ | $0.2$ |\n| **High-Cylinder** | $0.4$ | $0.8$ |  |\n\nThat is, $40%$ of classic motorcycle customers switch to high-cylinder each decade, and $20%$ of high-cylinder motorcycle customers switch to Classic.\n\nCurrently, $7\\,000$ customers use classic motorcycles while $3\\,000$ customers use high-cylinder motorcycles. How many customers will use high-cylinder motorcycles in one decade? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." 13194139535239 true] [17592186339175 1113 #uuid "3bc34242-e133-5fba-9823-018a7dbf9dab" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339175 1145 "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.6 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of motorcycle is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}7\\,000 \\\\ 3\\,000\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(10\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{10\\,000}[\\begin{aligned}7\\,000 \\\\ 3\\,000\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one decade, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.6 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.48 \\\\ 0.52\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one decade, $52%$ of customers will use high-cylinder motorcycles. Computing $52%$ of $10\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n10\\,000\\cdot 0.52=5\\,200.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5\\,200$ customers will use high-cylinder motorcycles in one decade." 13194139535239 true] [17592186339176 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339177 13194139535239 true] [17592186339176 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339178 13194139535239 true] [17592186339176 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339179 13194139535239 true] [17592186339176 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339180 13194139535239 true] [17592186339176 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339181 13194139535239 true] [17592186339176 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535239 true] [17592186339176 1041 "selection" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339176 1104 #edb/ref 17592186339181 13194139535239 true] [17592186339176 1110 #uuid "b4d86fbd-2ba8-5a9e-af75-a6c013906351" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339177 1034 "6\\,400" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339177 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535239 true] [17592186339177 1111 #uuid "98ec2983-2653-562b-a96e-863ff082b2da" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339178 1034 "6\\,100" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339178 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535239 true] [17592186339178 1111 #uuid "c780875d-94ba-5678-9842-a32cfbce6f6e" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339179 1034 "4\\,800" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339179 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535239 true] [17592186339179 1111 #uuid "e8b75824-49e3-5879-99a4-1b2847a7cc79" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339180 1034 "5\\,600" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339180 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535239 true] [17592186339180 1111 #uuid "00566601-c5f6-5ca2-9cf6-d95445c8aa2e" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339181 1034 "5\\,200" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339181 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535239 true] [17592186339181 1111 #uuid "f5fab232-a7bc-5b75-a91b-ec8e0cd8a3ab" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339182 1017 #edb/ref 17592186339183 13194139535239 true] [17592186339182 1089 "q-135460" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339182 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535239 true] [17592186339182 1100 "A certain company produces three types of milk: whole, skim, and lactose-free. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between the three types of milk each year:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Whole** | **Skim** | **Lactose-free** |\n| To: | **Whole** | $0.5$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |\n| **Skim** | $0.3$ | $0.4$ | $0.4$ |  |\n| **Lactose-free** | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |  |\n\nFor example, $30%$ of whole milk customers switch to skim milk each year, and $20%$ of lactose-free milk customers switch to whole milk.\n\nCurrently, $400\\,000$ customers consume whole milk, $400\\,000$ customers consume skim milk, and $200\\,000$ customers consume lactose-free milk. How many customers will consume whole milk after one year? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." 13194139535239 true] [17592186339182 1113 #uuid "c83a21c4-20b5-5d81-82df-2c0054d8944c" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339182 1145 "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.3 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3 & 0.4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of milk is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}400\\,000 \\\\ 400\\,000 \\\\ 200\\,000\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{1\\,000\\,000}[\\begin{aligned}400\\,000 \\\\ 400\\,000 \\\\ 200\\,000\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 \\\\ 0.4 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.3 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3 & 0.4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.4 \\\\ 0.4 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.36 \\\\ 0.36 \\\\ 0.28\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one year, $36%$ of customers will consume whole milk. Computing $36%$ of $1\\,000\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.36=360\\,000.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $360\\,000$ customers will consume whole milk after one year." 13194139535239 true] [17592186339183 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339184 13194139535239 true] [17592186339183 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339185 13194139535239 true] [17592186339183 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339186 13194139535239 true] [17592186339183 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339187 13194139535239 true] [17592186339183 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339188 13194139535239 true] [17592186339183 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535239 true] [17592186339183 1041 "selection" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339183 1104 #edb/ref 17592186339188 13194139535239 true] [17592186339183 1110 #uuid "7f548f38-d37c-5a8d-8446-19cedf6297ee" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339184 1034 "320\\,000" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339184 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535239 true] [1