doesn't change." 13194139535239 true] [17592186339189 1113 #uuid "04b428ea-6569-5051-b764-669659243c1d" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339189 1145 "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.2 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.6 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & 0.4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of ground coffee is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}300\\,000 \\\\ 500\\,000 \\\\ 200\\,000\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{1\\,000\\,000}[\\begin{aligned}300\\,000 \\\\ 500\\,000 \\\\ 200\\,000\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.3 \\\\ 0.5 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.2 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.6 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & 0.4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.3 \\\\ 0.5 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.29 \\\\ 0.47 \\\\ 0.24\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one year, $29%$ of customers will buy fine ground coffee. Computing $29%$ of $1\\,000\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.29=290\\,000.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $290\\,000$ customers will buy fine ground coffee in one year." 13194139535239 true] [17592186339190 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339191 13194139535239 true] [17592186339190 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339192 13194139535239 true] [17592186339190 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339193 13194139535239 true] [17592186339190 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339194 13194139535239 true] [17592186339190 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339195 13194139535239 true] [17592186339190 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535239 true] [17592186339190 1041 "selection" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339190 1104 #edb/ref 17592186339195 13194139535239 true] [17592186339190 1110 #uuid "237c83fd-10f6-5c94-9c97-1a878eb8b293" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339191 1034 "340\\,000" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339191 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535239 true] [17592186339191 1111 #uuid "2c802ab6-a774-55a2-ba3a-78dca2c87b59" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339192 1034 "240\\,000" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339192 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535239 true] [17592186339192 1111 #uuid "f837d30e-5eb2-51d0-a063-30752cd48247" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339193 1034 "470\\,000" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339193 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535239 true] [17592186339193 1111 #uuid "f3ffd6c5-f462-513c-a984-0b636995dc1f" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339194 1034 "390\\,000" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339194 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535239 true] [17592186339194 1111 #uuid "38ca66e9-278f-535f-8e35-8f246237a5ed" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339195 1034 "290\\,000" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339195 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535239 true] [17592186339195 1111 #uuid "3a881244-9a4d-5fb4-acdf-d84267e255c2" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339196 1017 #edb/ref 17592186339197 13194139535239 true] [17592186339196 1089 "q-135466" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339196 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535239 true] [17592186339196 1100 "A certain cinema sells three types of popcorn: traditional, butter, and caramelized. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between the three types of popcorn each month:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Traditional** | **Butter** | **Caramelized** |\n| To: | **Traditional** | $0.4$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n| **Butter** | $0.3$ | $0.5$ | $0.2$ |  |\n| **Caramelized** | $0.3$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ |  |\n\nFor example, $30%$ of traditional popcorn customers switch to butter popcorn each month, and $40%$ of butter popcorn customers switch to caramelized popcorn.\n\nCurrently, $400$ customers buy traditional popcorn, $300$ customers buy butter popcorn, and $300$ customers buy caramelized popcorn. How many customers will purchase butter popcorn in one month? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." 13194139535239 true] [17592186339196 1113 #uuid "675dc848-9bb3-55cc-acc9-5f6cc9b0d9c6" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339196 1145 "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.5 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of popcorn customers is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}400 \\\\ 300 \\\\ 300\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{1\\,000}[\\begin{aligned}400 \\\\ 300 \\\\ 300\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 \\\\ 0.3 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.5 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.4 \\\\ 0.3 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.25 \\\\ 0.33 \\\\ 0.42\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one year, $33%$ of customers will buy butter popcorn. Computing $33%$ of $1\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000\\cdot 0.33=330.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $330$ customers will buy butter popcorn in one month." 13194139535239 true] [17592186339197 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339198 13194139535239 true] [17592186339197 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339199 13194139535239 true] [17592186339197 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339200 13194139535239 true] [17592186339197 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339201 13194139535239 true] [17592186339197 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339202 13194139535239 true] [17592186339197 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535239 true] [17592186339197 1041 "selection" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339197 1104 #edb/ref 17592186339200 13194139535239 true] [17592186339197 1110 #uuid "d09405d1-0c7d-5393-affa-682c82829d3f" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339198 1034 "290" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339198 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535239 true] [17592186339198 1111 #uuid "57f41175-8631-53ca-905d-1aa815f840bc" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339199 1034 "420" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339199 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535239 true] [17592186339199 1111 #uuid "8e53e619-5d0d-5c44-80e7-cc6eb47f044a" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339200 1034 "330" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339200 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535239 true] [17592186339200 1111 #uuid "86f475a4-9810-5305-a243-fbd7527ce134" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339201 1034 "310" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339201 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535239 true] [17592186339201 1111 #uuid "d0d6ac7b-eeb0-54b6-b3aa-2f177bb4c9f0" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339202 1034 "360" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339202 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535239 true] [17592186339202 1111 #uuid "12f265fc-3789-5bc9-8fce-070d1d0bbf98" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339203 1017 #edb/ref 17592186339204 13194139535239 true] [17592186339203 1089 "q-135483" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339203 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535239 true] [17592186339203 1100 "Which of the following are stochastic vectors?\n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}0.45 \\\\ 0.55 \\\\ -0.05\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0.65 \\\\ 0.15 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339203 1113 #uuid "60493d3a-a1d2-50c0-a800-38f0bca06ee2" 13194139535239 true] [17592186339203 1145 "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our vectors in turn.\n\n- Vector $u$ is *not* a stochastic vector since it contains negative entries.\n\n- Vector $v$ is a 